WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.335
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.203 --> 00:00:07.640
DYANN F. WIRTH: Malaria
is a complex organism.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.810
It's a eukaryotic pathogen.
It has over 5,000 genes.

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:15.470
It exists in the insect vector.

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:20.030
And in the human host, it exists
in the liver and in the red cell.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:26.540
Malaria eradication means eliminating
an organism from the face of the Earth.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:31.430
And to do that, we need to understand
the biology of the organism

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:36.050
in order to develop the
necessary tools, drugs, vaccines,

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:37.490
and other interventions.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:43.130
In addition, the parasite's ability
to adapt to external challenges,

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:47.390
to drugs, to the human immune
system, to the mosquito vector

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:50.270
make it a fascinating biological system.

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:53.640
KENT KESTER: Parasites have
evolved with us for millennia.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:54.860
They know how to hide out.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:56.270
They know how to live with us.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:01:01.340
DYANN F. WIRTH: The course brings
together people from malaria control

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.220
programs, from
nongovernmental organizations,

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:08.060
from academic institutions
to work together

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.690
to develop the skills that are
needed to lead this effort.

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:14.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

